)\n   641\t             (set? a) (every? (fn [e]\n   642\t                                (same-meta? e (some #(when (= e %) %) b)))\n   643\t                              a)\n   644\t             (sequential? a) (every? true? (map same-meta? a b))\n   645\t             :else true)))\n   646\t\n   647\t\n   648\t(defn ast->semantic-bytecode\n   649\t  \"Project a map AST to flat content-addressed rows (§6.1 codec boundary\n   650\t   projection). Returns `{:root row-id, :rows {row-id [row-id tag & slots]}}`.\n   651\t\n   652\t   Only the §2.3 slots of each node enter its row: `:eid`, `:macro?`,\n   653\t   `:yang/*` keys, non-`:application` `:tail?` marks, and any other key are\n   654\t   dropped. `sym`/`syms` slots lose all metadata; `data`/`key` payloads lose\n   655\t   reader positions at every depth and keep all other metadata. Defaults are\n   656\t   saturated per §2.4. Children are projected first, so a parent's body\n   657\t   commits to its children's ids; structurally identical subtrees mint one\n   658\t   id and are stored as one row (§4.4).\n   659\t\n   660\t   Known limitation, inherited from `dao.jing` and not fixed here: the\n   661\t   transitional encoder does not hash metadata on scalars, so two payloads\n   662\t   differing only in retained symbol metadata (e.g. `^{:meaning 1} x` and\n   663\t   `^{:meaning 2} x` as literal values) mint one address. Such a collision\n   664\t   throws `\\\"Semantic bytecode address collision\\\"` rather than letting one\n   665\t   row's metadata silently replace the other's. It disappears when\n   666\t   `dao.jing`'s encoding covers scalar metadata.\"\n   667\t  [ast]\n   668\t  (let [rows (atom {})]\n   669\t    (letfn\n   670\t      [(slot-value\n   671\t         [tag node [field kind]]\n   672\t         (let [v (get node field)\n   673\t               v (if (nil? v) (get semantic-bytecode-defaults [tag field]) v)]\n   674\t           (case kind\n   675\t             :node (convert v)\n   676\t         