{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"## Findings\n\n1. **Blocking | [v2.cljc:636](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:636) | Metadata nested inside metadata still merges silently.**\n\n   `same-meta?` compares metadata with `=`, without recursively inspecting that metadata. I reproduced the failure using these literal values in two branches:\n   ```clojure\n   (with-meta [] {:note (with-meta 'x {:meaning 1})})\n   (with-meta [] {:note (with-meta 'x {:meaning 2})})\n   ```\n   They receive the same address, the guard accepts them, and both reconstructed values contain `{:meaning 2}` on the nested symbol.\n\n   **Fix:** Recursively compare metadata itself, including metadata on its keys and values. This leaves r1 finding 2 open.\n\n2. **Blocking | [v2.cljc:623](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:623) | The new stripping function corrupts map-entry payloads.**\n\n   Verified:\n   ```clojure\n   {:type :literal :value (first {:a 1})}\n   ```\n   The input value is `[:a 1]`; reconstruction returns `(1 :a)`. Both value equality and the original body’s address change.\n\n   A Clojure map entry satisfies `vector?` and `plain-data?`, but `(empty entry)` returns nil. Consequently, `into` builds a reversed list.\n\n   **Fix:** Handle map entries explicitly; do not assume every `vector?` value has a vector-valued `empty`. Add this case to both round-trip tests.\n\n3. **Blocking | [v2.cljc:628](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:628) | Reader-position stripping does not traverse metadata.**\n\n   A literal containing:\n   ```clojure\n   (with-meta [] {:note (with-meta 'x {:line 77})})\n   ```\n   still carries `{:line 77}` inside its emitted row. Only the outer metadata map’s position keys are removed.\n\n   **Fix:** Recursively sanitize retained metadata, including metadata-bearing values and metadata on the metadata map itself. R1 finding 3 is only partially fixed.\n\n4. **Blocking | [v2.cljc:642](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:642) | Set matching can reject an identical value.**\n\n   Ordinary hash sets cannot contain two elements differing only in metadata. A custom sorted set can:\n   ```clojure\n   (sorted-set-by\n     #(compare (:id (meta %1)) (:id (meta %2)))\n     (with-meta 'x {:id 1})\n     (with-meta 'x {:id 2}))\n   ```\n   This set contains two `=`-equal symbols. I verified that `(same-meta? s s)` returns false and that two identical literal occurrences throw an address-collision exception. The scan repeatedly selects the first equal element.\n\n   **Fix:** Match elements one-to-one using the complete metadata-sensitive comparison, or explicitly validate and reject comparator-defined collections outside the supported domain. For ordinary maps, reordered entries matched correctly through `find`; that does not establish correctness for arbitrary custom comparators.\n\n5. **Blocking | [v2.cljc:726](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:726) | Accepted `:syms` rows can still change addresses.**\n\n   A correctly hashed lambda row whose params are:\n   ```clojure\n   (with-meta ['x] {:purpose 1})\n   ```\n   passes reconstruction. Re-projection removes the vector metadata through `mapv`, producing a different address. The validator checks element types but not the canonical metadata constraints enforced by projection.\n\n   **Fix:** Reject noncanonical metadata in reconstructed `sym`/`syms` slots, or define preservation consistently in both directions. Namespaced symbols round-trip correctly; nil, lists, and mixed non-symbol vectors are now rejected as intended.\n\n## Other requested checks\n\n- **Records:** A direct record payload still reaches `dao.jing` rejection. I verified the same exception message and ex-data as direct hashing. That behavior was not swallowed.\n- **Byte arrays:** Stripping returns the same array unchanged. However, the current §2.2 `plain-data?` predicate **rejects byte arrays**. `dao.jing.md` describes broader intended support and explicitly documents the current identity-based hashing limitation. Two content-equal arrays produced different addresses.\n- **Address neutrality:** Ordinary reader-position stripping is address-neutral under the default printer binding, but “never changes any address” is too strong. The map-entry regression changes addresses, and binding `*print-meta*` to true makes the current encoder hash reader metadata on symbols.\n- **Unreachable rows:** The r1 finding remains explicitly unimplemented. It was part of the original blocking finding 4, not merely a newly introduced follow-up.\n\n## Verification and verdict\n\nIndependently confirmed **19 tests / 150 assertions / 0 failures**, then reproduced the failures above using read-only JVM probes. No files were edited; CLJS and CLJD were not rerun.\n\n| Original blocker | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Grammar kind mismatch | Closed |\n| Metadata-sharing corruption | Still open |\n| Reader-provenance exclusion | Partially fixed |\n| Accepted rows silently changing | Partially fixed |\n\n**Verdict: No.** The four original blockers are not all closed, and the stripping implementation introduces a new value-corruption defect. This is not ready for Architect sign-off."}}
